detail.abstract:
Appearance shapes social perception prior to speech, credentials, or action. Across cultural contexts, involuntary visible physical traits influence judgments of competence, morality, desirability, and social belonging. Yet despite decades of research on body image, stigma, visible difference, and health inequality, appearance has not been conceptualized as a population-level exposure variable within a structured epidemiological framework.&nbsp;
<div>
 This paper introduces Appearance Epidemiology (AE) as an interdisciplinary field concerned with the distribution, determinants, and consequences of involuntary visible physical traits that deviate from dominant population norms and generate patterned institutional and structural responses. AE reframes appearance from an individualized psychosocial concern to a measurable determinant operating across cultural, institutional, and structural systems.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The paper proposes the APi Model, a five-layer analytic framework specifying appearance exposure, psychosocial mediation, cultural norm formation, institutional conversion (lookism), and structural reproduction. Drawing from stigma theory (Goffman, 1963; Link &amp; Phelan, 2001), social determinants of health (Marmot, 2005), ecosocial theory (Krieger, 2001), structural violence (Farmer, 2004), and intersectionality (Crenshaw, 1989; Collins, 2000), AE isolates involuntary visible traits as structured exposure variables capable of population-level measurement.
</div>
<div>
 In contemporary image-saturated digital environments, where algorithmic systems amplify normative appearance standards and visual self-presentation increasingly mediates economic and social opportunity, the absence of a structured analytic framework for appearance-based inequality becomes increasingly consequential. Labor market and psychosocial penalties associated with visible differences remain largely unmonitored in inequality surveillance systems despite accumulating evidence of cumulative disadvantage across the life course. AE responds to this historical moment.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The paper further proposes the Global Appearance Inequality Index (GAIIx) as a flagship measurement initiative to operationalize the field and enable policy benchmarking.&nbsp;
</div>
